{0 Tutorial}

This tutorial makes two components. The first is a counter. The second is a
component that asks the server for a greeting with a Remote call.

The code of this page is in [docs/examples/] of the repository. Dune compiles
it for the server (native) and for the browser (Melange). A test makes sure
that the code on this page is the same as those files, and runs the examples
on this page.

{1 Part 1: a counter}

{2 The machine}

A component is a machine and a view. The machine holds all the behaviour. The
view shows the snapshot of the machine and sends events to it.

The counter has one state, [Count], which carries an [int]. The type of the
states is a GADT: each constructor gives the type of the data that the state
carries. [Machine.Make] makes the functions to build the machine for these
states.

The machine has two events. [Increment] goes to [Count] with the number plus
one. [Decrement] goes to [Count] with the number minus one, and stays when the
number is 0. The view is written in mlx, the JSX syntax for OCaml.

{@mlx file=examples/shared/tutorial_counter.mlx[
open Silica

type event = Increment | Decrement

module States = struct
  type _ t = Count : int t
end

module Counter_machine = Machine.Make (States)

let machine =
  Counter_machine.make ~id:"counter"
    ~init:(Counter_machine.at Count 0)
    [
      Counter_machine.state Count "count" (fun n -> function
        | Increment ->
            Counter_machine.goto Count (n + 1)
        | Decrement when n = 0 ->
            Counter_machine.stay []
        | Decrement ->
            Counter_machine.goto Count (n - 1)
        );
    ]

let view (Counter_machine.At (Count, n) : Counter_machine.snapshot) :
    event Node.t =
  <div>
    <button on_press=Decrement disabled=(n = 0)>"Decrement"</button>
    <span>(Node.text (string_of_int n))</span>
    <button on_press=Increment>"Increment"</button>
  </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

{2 Run the machine}

A machine is a plain value. You can start it and send events to it without a
browser. [Machine.start] gives the first snapshot. [Machine.send] gives the
next snapshot and the commands that the host must run.

{@ocaml[
# let counter = Tutorial_counter.machine;;
val counter :
  (Tutorial_counter.Counter_machine.snapshot, Tutorial_counter.event)
  Silica.Machine.t = <abstr>
# let snapshot, _ = Silica.Machine.start counter;;
val snapshot : Tutorial_counter.Counter_machine.snapshot =
  Tutorial_counter.Counter_machine.At (Tutorial_counter.States.Count, <poly>)
# let snapshot, _, _ =
    Silica.Machine.send counter snapshot Tutorial_counter.Increment;;
val snapshot : Tutorial_counter.Counter_machine.snapshot =
  Tutorial_counter.Counter_machine.At (Tutorial_counter.States.Count, <poly>)
]}

[Render.to_string] makes the HTML of a view. The server uses it for the first
paint of a page.

{@ocaml[
# Silica.Render.to_string (Tutorial_counter.view snapshot);;
- : string =
"<div><button>Decrement</button><span>1</span><button>Increment</button></div>"
]}

{2 Check the machine}

The checker ([silica.check]) runs every event in every state that it can
reach, and checks properties of each view. For example, each button must have
a name that a screen reader can read. The counter can count forever, so
[within] limits the search to the numbers 0 to 3.

{@ocaml[
# let within
      (Tutorial_counter.Counter_machine.At (Count, n) :
        Tutorial_counter.Counter_machine.snapshot) =
    n <= 3;;
val within : Tutorial_counter.Counter_machine.snapshot -> bool = <fun>
# match Check.component ~within Tutorial_counter.component with
  | Check.Ok { states; _ } -> Printf.sprintf "%d states, no problem" states
  | _ -> "problem";;
- : string = "4 states, no problem"
]}

{2 Show it in the browser}

The browser package [silica.browser] mounts a component into an element of the
page. The {{!section-browser}second part} shows a complete client file.

{1:part2 Part 2: a machine with a Remote call}

{2 The endpoint}

A Remote call is one request to a typed endpoint on the server. Declare the
endpoint in code that the server and the browser share. The codecs change the
input and the output to JSON and back.

{@ocaml file=examples/shared/tutorial_api.ml[
let greeting =
  Silica.Remote.declare ~path:"/api/greeting" ~input:Silica.Codec.string
    ~output:Silica.Codec.string
]}

{2 The machine}

The greeting machine has four states. In [Idle], a press on the button sends
[Ask], and the machine goes to [Asking]. The state [Asking] owns the call: the
call starts when the machine enters the state, and its answer comes back as
the event [Answered]. If the machine leaves the state first, the answer is
dropped. The answer takes the machine to [Shown] or to [Failed].

[to_] lists the states that a handler can go to. The checker uses this list to
find edges that no event takes.

{@mlx file=examples/shared/tutorial_greeting.mlx[
open Silica

type event = Ask | Answered of (string, Remote.error) result

module States = struct
  type _ t =
    | Idle : unit t
    | Asking : unit t
    | Shown : string t
    | Failed : Remote.error t
end

module Greeting_machine = Machine.Make (States)

let ask = function
  | Ask ->
      Greeting_machine.goto Asking ()
  | Answered _ ->
      Greeting_machine.unhandled

let machine =
  Greeting_machine.make ~id:"greeting"
    ~init:(Greeting_machine.at Idle ())
    [
      Greeting_machine.state Idle "idle" ~to_:[ Asking ] (fun () -> ask);
      Greeting_machine.state Asking "asking" ~to_:[ Shown; Failed ]
        ~invoke:
          [
            Source.call Tutorial_api.greeting
              (fun () -> "Ada")
              (fun result -> Answered result);
          ]
        (fun () -> function
          | Answered (Ok text) ->
              Greeting_machine.goto Shown text
          | Answered (Error error) ->
              Greeting_machine.goto Failed error
          | Ask ->
              Greeting_machine.unhandled
          );
      Greeting_machine.state Shown "shown" ~to_:[ Asking ] (fun _ -> ask);
      Greeting_machine.state Failed "failed" ~to_:[ Asking ] (fun _ -> ask);
    ]

let view (snapshot : Greeting_machine.snapshot) : event Node.t =
  match snapshot with
  | Greeting_machine.At (Idle, ()) ->
      <button on_press=Ask>"Say hello"</button>
  | Greeting_machine.At (Asking, ()) ->
      <p>"Asking the server."</p>
  | Greeting_machine.At (Shown, text) ->
      <div>
        <p>(Node.text text)</p> <button on_press=Ask>"Ask again"</button>
      </div>
  | Greeting_machine.At (Failed, _) ->
      <div>
        <p>"The server did not answer."</p>
        <button on_press=Ask>"Try again"</button>
      </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

{2 Run the machine}

When the machine enters [Asking], [Machine.send] gives a [Start_call] command.
The host runs the call. The test below does not run it, so the machine stays in
[Asking].

{@ocaml[
# let greeting = Tutorial_greeting.machine;;
val greeting :
  (Tutorial_greeting.Greeting_machine.snapshot, Tutorial_greeting.event)
  Silica.Machine.t = <abstr>
# let snapshot, _ = Silica.Machine.start greeting;;
val snapshot : Tutorial_greeting.Greeting_machine.snapshot =
  Tutorial_greeting.Greeting_machine.At (Tutorial_greeting.States.Idle,
   <poly>)
# let snapshot, commands, _ =
    Silica.Machine.send greeting snapshot Tutorial_greeting.Ask;;
val snapshot : Tutorial_greeting.Greeting_machine.snapshot =
  Tutorial_greeting.Greeting_machine.At (Tutorial_greeting.States.Asking,
   <poly>)
val commands : Tutorial_greeting.event Silica.Cmd.host list =
  [Silica.Cmd.Start_call
    {Silica.Cmd.id = "greeting/asking/invoke0"; endpoint = <abstr>;
     input = <poly>; timeout_ms = None; on_result = <fun>}]
# Silica.Machine.key greeting snapshot;;
- : string = "asking"
]}

The machine gets the answer as an event, as any other event:

{@ocaml[
# let snapshot, _, _ =
    Silica.Machine.send greeting snapshot
      (Tutorial_greeting.Answered (Ok "Hello, Ada"));;
val snapshot : Tutorial_greeting.Greeting_machine.snapshot =
  Tutorial_greeting.Greeting_machine.At (Tutorial_greeting.States.Shown,
   <poly>)
# Silica.Render.to_string (Tutorial_greeting.view snapshot);;
- : string = "<div><p>Hello, Ada</p><button>Ask again</button></div>"
]}

{2 Check the machine}

The checker gives the call each answer in [answers], and also the errors
[Http 500] and [Network "down"]. So it checks the success path and the error
paths.

{@ocaml[
# match
    Check.component
      ~samples:[ Check.answers Tutorial_api.greeting [ "Hello, Ada" ] ]
      Tutorial_greeting.component
  with
  | Check.Ok { states; _ } -> Printf.sprintf "%d states, no problem" states
  | _ -> "problem";;
- : string = "5 states, no problem"
]}

{2:browser The browser}

The client file mounts the component into the element with the id
[silica-root]. Dune compiles it with Melange, and a bundler such as esbuild
makes the file for the page.

{@ocaml file=examples/client/tutorial_client.ml[
let () = Silica_browser.mount ~root:"silica-root" Tutorial_greeting.component
]}

{2 The server}

The server answers the endpoint and renders the page. See
{!page-recipes.hosting} for the file, and {!page-server} for what the server
does.

